## Proof

We evaluate
$$S=\sum_{k=1}^{\infty}\frac{(-1)^{k-1}}{k}\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\frac{1}{k\,2^{n}+1}.$$

**Step 1 — Integral representation of the inner sum.**

For $k\ge 1$ and $n\ge 0$,
$$\frac{1}{k\,2^{n}+1}=\int_{0}^{1}x^{k\,2^{n}}\,dx.$$
Hence
$$\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\frac{1}{k\,2^{n}+1}=\int_{0}^{1}\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}x^{k\,2^{n}}\,dx,$$
where the interchange is justified since $x^{k2^{n}}\le x^{2^{n}}$ and $\sum_{n}x^{2^{n}}$ converges for $x\in[0,1)$ (super-exponential decay), with the $x=1$ endpoint harmless.

**Step 2 — Sum over $k$ via the logarithm series.**

Substituting and exchanging the $k$- and $n$-sums (justified by absolute convergence: $\sum_{k,n}\frac{x^{k2^{n}}}{k}\le\sum_{n}\frac{x^{2^{n}}}{1-x^{2^{n}}}<\infty$ on $[0,1)$),
$$S=\int_{0}^{1}\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\left(\sum_{k=1}^{\infty}\frac{(-1)^{k-1}}{k}\,x^{k\,2^{n}}\right)dx.$$
Using $\sum_{k=1}^{\infty}\frac{(-1)^{k-1}}{k}z^{k}=\ln(1+z)$ for $|z|\le 1$, $z\neq -1$,
$$S=\int_{0}^{1}\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\ln\!\bigl(1+x^{2^{n}}\bigr)\,dx.$$

**Step 3 — The infinite product identity.**

The classical telescoping product gives, for $x\in[0,1)$,
$$(1-x)\prod_{n=0}^{N}\bigl(1+x^{2^{n}}\bigr)=(1-x)(1+x)(1+x^{2})\cdots\bigl(1+x^{2^{N}}\bigr)=1-x^{2^{N+1}}.$$
Letting $N\to\infty$,
$$\prod_{n=0}^{\infty}\bigl(1+x^{2^{n}}\bigr)=\frac{1}{1-x},\qquad x\in[0,1).$$
Taking logarithms,
$$\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\ln\!\bigl(1+x^{2^{n}}\bigr)=-\ln(1-x),\qquad x\in[0,1).$$

**Step 4 — Passing the limit through the integral.**

The partial products $\prod_{n=0}^{N}(1+x^{2^{n}})=\frac{1-x^{2^{N+1}}}{1-x}$ increase to $\frac{1}{1-x}$, so the partial sums of logarithms increase to $-\ln(1-x)$. By the monotone convergence theorem,
$$S=\int_{0}^{1}\lim_{N\to\infty}\sum_{n=0}^{N}\ln\!\bigl(1+x^{2^{n}}\bigr)\,dx=\int_{0}^{1}\bigl(-\ln(1-x)\bigr)\,dx.$$

**Step 5 — Final evaluation.**

With $u=1-x$,
$$S=\int_{0}^{1}(-\ln u)\,du=\Bigl[u-u\ln u\Bigr]_{0}^{1}=1-0=1,$$
using $\lim_{u\to 0^{+}}u\ln u=0$.

---

$$\boxed{S=1}$$

